PATCH 501: the player-death camera hand-off -- 5087 and 5fca base-loss.

Both are reached only from FUN_0000_a93e's `di == word[DGROUP:0x6d34]` branch, i.e. the frame on which
the PLAYER object is destroyed, so neither could run before patches 499/500 unblocked the impact cascade
and got a93e that far.

FUN_0000_5087 (asm 0x5087-0x50a1) latches the camera-anchor object's X/Y:
    5088: 8b 3e e0 7a   mov di,[0x7ae0]      ; an object NEAR OFFSET (ab03 compares DI against it)
    508e: 74 10         je  0x50a0
    5090: 66 8b 45 04   mov eax,[di+4]  ; 5094: 66 a3 84 7b  mov [0x7b84],eax
    5098: 66 8b 45 08   mov eax,[di+8]  ; 509c: 66 a3 88 7b  mov [0x7b88],eax
Ghidra dereferenced word[0x7ae0] as a host pointer.

FUN_1000_5fca (asm 0x15fca-0x16014) hands the camera to the next friendly vehicle: it walks the
DGROUP:0x6d3c WORD cell table -- the same table as patches 264 and 500 -- starting at the dead object's
own cell group (`movzbw bx,[di+0x1b]; shl bx,3`), stepping 2 bytes and wrapping at 0x40, for at most
0x20 entries, and takes the first live non-wreck candidate on the SAME side (`[si+0x16]&8 == [di+0x16]&8`)
-> 466c (which already takes the near offset, patch 300) + bedc; if none, 5cce.
Ghidra typed the table `int *` -- indexing it with a byte offset while reading 4 bytes per entry -- and
dereferenced every near offset as a host pointer (`*piVar1 != 0x17`, `piVar1[0xb]`).

Measured: with 500 in, AZER1/AZER3/AZER5/AZER6/CYPRUS1 SIGSEGV'd at 5087 <- a93e <- b39c <- c336 <-
c31e <- bbb7; 5fca is the very next call in the same sequence and carries the same defect.

Noted honestly: AX at 5fca's 0x16000 exit is (ah<<8)|al, and AH is written only inside the loop, so on
the path where no candidate is ever examined it is a dead input; it is modelled as 0.
